  "question": {
    "text": "Let the relations $R_1$ and $R_2$ on the set $X=\\{1,2,3, \\ldots, 20\\}$ be given by $R_1=\\{(x, y): 2 x-3 y=2\\}$ and $R_2=\\{(x, y):-5 x+4 y=0\\}$. If $M$ and $N$ be the minimum number of elements required to be added in $R_1$ and $R_2$, respectively, in order to make the relations symmetric, then $M+N$ equals",
    "image": null
